course: dictionary
lesson: 9497
title: Stencil: a shape you can copy
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A stencil is a thin sheet of card, plastic or metal with shapes or letters cut out of it. You lay it on a surface, paint through the holes, and lift it off to leave the same shape behind. Stencil can also be a verb: to stencil is to make a pattern this way.
explain: «People also blew paint around their hands to leave hand outlines, called stencils, which are found all over the world.» Here stencils are the hand pictures on the walls, not the tool. Context tells you which meaning is used: the tool, the action, or the picture it makes.
explain: Stencils help you make neat, copied shapes. In art you use them for patterns and letters, and in maths shape stencils help you draw neat polygons and circles. «Banksy's stencils» are famous street art, and they often joke about war, money and power.
fact: A stencil is a thin sheet of card, plastic or metal with shapes or letters cut out of it.
fact: To stencil means to make a pattern or letters by painting through the holes.
fact: A stencil can also mean the picture that was made using it.
fact: Cave people made hand stencils by blowing paint around their hands on cave walls, some of the oldest art we know.
fact: Banksy is a British street artist whose name and face are kept secret, and his stencils are famous.
exercise: What is a stencil? | A brush with soft hairs | A wall covered in graffiti | *A thin sheet with shapes or letters cut out of it | A tin with a lid for paint
exercise: Ravi wants the same star shape ten times on his poster. Which tool helps him? | A pencil with no eraser | *A stencil | A box of crayons | A ruler
exercise: Which word means to make a pattern by painting through holes? | Sketch | *Stencil | Glue | Carve
test: In «People also blew paint around their hands to leave hand outlines, called stencils», what are the stencils? | The brushes they held | The caves themselves | The paint pots the cave people used | *The hand pictures left by blowing paint around the hand
test: In maths, what can shape stencils help you draw? | Huge murals on a wall | Stories about animals | *Neat polygons and circles | Long sentences
exam: Which sentence uses stencil as a verb? | The stencil was made of thin card | *Ali stencilled his name onto his bag | The stencil lay under the paper | Ali hung the stencil on the wall
